 gni:`<div class="rule"><b>Slope</b> m = rise/run = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁).</div><p>Positive slope rises left to right. Negative falls. Zero is flat. A vertical line has undefined slope (you’d divide by zero).</p><div class="ex">(1, 2) and (4, 11)<br>m = (11 − 2)/(4 − 1) = 9/3 = 3</div>`,
 dev:'Slope is a rate: km per hour, dB per km, gallons per hour, dollars per month. Reading a rate off a graph or a table is reading slope.',
 gen(){const x1=ri(-8,8),y1=ri(-8,8);let x2;do{x2=ri(-8,8)}while(x2===x1);const y2=ri(-8,8);
  return {q:`Find the slope through (${N(x1)}, ${N(y1)}) and (${N(x2)}, ${N(y2)}).`,type:'num',ans:(y2-y1)/(x2-x1),tol:.001,sol:`m = (${N(y2)} − ${P(y1)})/(${N(x2)} − ${P(x1)}) = ${N(y2-y1)}/${N(x2-x1)} = <b>${frac(y2-y1,x2-x1)}</b>.`};}},
{id:'line',title:'Writing linear equations',bloom:'Apply',
 hint:'Find m first, then b = y₁ − m·x₁. Perpendicular slopes are negative reciprocals.',
 ce:{text:'A tower climber goes at a steady rate. At 3 minutes he’s at 50 ft. At 7 minutes he’s at 110 ft. Write height as h = mt + b.',item:{q:'Find m and b.',type:'fields',fields:[{label:'m (ft per min)',ans:15},{label:'b (starting height)',ans:5}],sol:'m = (110 − 50)/(7 − 3) = 15. b = 50 − 15·3 = 5. <b>h = 15t + 5</b>. He started on a 5 ft platform.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule"><b>Slope-intercept:</b> y = mx + b (b is where it crosses the y-axis).<br><b>Point-slope:</b> y − y₁ = m(x − x₁).</div><div class="rule">Parallel lines have the same m. Perpendicular slopes multiply to −1, so m₂ = −1/m₁.</div><div class="ex">Through (2, 7) and (5, 13)<br>m = 6/3 = 2<br>b = 7 − 2·2 = 3<br>y = 2x + 3</div><p>Type the right side, like <code>2x+3</code>.</p>`,
 dev:'Two data points are enough to build a model and predict. That’s how you’d estimate time to complete, cost at volume, or drift over time.',
 gen(d){const b=ri(-9,9);
  if(d<3){const m=nz(-5,5),x1=ri(-5,5);let x2;do{x2=ri(-5,5)}while(x2===x1);const y1=m*x1+b,y2=m*x2+b;
   if(d===1)return {q:`Write the line with slope ${N(m)} through (${N(x1)}, ${N(y1)}). Form: y = mx + b`,type:'expr',ans:`${m}*x+(${b})`,sol:`b = ${N(y1)} − ${P(m)}·${P(x1)} = ${N(b)}. <b>y = ${poly([m,b])}</b>.`};
   return {q:`Write the line through (${N(x1)}, ${N(y1)}) and (${N(x2)}, ${N(y2)}). Form: y = mx + b`,type:'expr',ans:`${m}*x+(${b})`,sol:`m = (${N(y2)} − ${P(y1)})/(${N(x2)} − ${P(x1)}) = ${N(m)}. b = ${N(y1)} − ${P(m)}·${P(x1)} = ${N(b)}. <b>y = ${poly([m,b])}</b>.`};}
  const m=nz(-3,3),c=ri(-9,9),x1=ri(-4,4),y1=m*x1+b,g=Math.abs(m)===1?(m>0?M:'')+'x':`(${frac(-1,m)})x`;
  return {q:`Line L is y = ${g}${sg(c)}. Write the line perpendicular to L through (${N(x1)}, ${N(y1)}). Form: y = mx + b`,type:'expr',ans:`${m}*x+(${b})`,sol:`Negative reciprocal of ${frac(-1,m)} is ${N(m)}. b = ${N(y1)} − ${P(m)}·${P(x1)} = ${N(b)}. <b>y = ${poly([m,b])}</b>.`};}},
{id:'func',title:'Functions, domain and range',bloom:'Understand',
 hint:'f(k) means substitute k for x. Domain: no dividing by zero, no square roots of negatives.',
 ce:{text:'Antenna planning: λ(f) = 300/f gives wavelength in meters for a frequency f in MHz. What’s the wavelength at 150 MHz?',item:{q:'λ(150) = ?',type:'num',ans:2,sol:'300/150 = <b>2 m</b>. A half-wave dipole for that frequency is about 1 m.'}},
 gni:`<p>A <b>function</b> gives exactly one output for each input. f(x) is the output when the input is x.</p><div class="rule"><b>Domain</b> = inputs allowed. Exclude anything that divides by zero or takes an even root of a negative. <b>Range</b> = outputs you can get.</div><div class="ex">f(x) = x² − 3x → f(−2) = 4 + 6 = 10<br>√(x − 4): domain x ≥ 4<br>1/(x + 2): domain x ≠ −2</div>`,
 dev:'Functions are how you think about inputs and outputs: frequency in, wavelength out; hours in, fuel out. The domain is the set of inputs that make physical sense.',
 gen(d){const f=pick(d===1?['ev','solve']:['ev','dom','solve']);
  if(f==='ev'){const a=nz(-3,3),b=ri(-6,6),c=ri(-9,9),k=nz(-4,4),v=a*k*k+b*k+c;return {q:`f(x) = ${poly([a,b,c])}. Find f(${N(k)}).`,type:'num',ans:v,sol:`${N(a)}·${P(k)}²${sg(b)}·${P(k)}${sg(c)} = <b>${N(v)}</b>.`};}
  if(f==='solve'){const m=nz(-6,6),b=nz(-9,9),x=ri(-8,8),v=m*x+b;return {q:`f(x) = ${poly([m,b])}. For what x is f(x) = ${N(v)}?`,type:'num',ans:x,sol:`${poly([m,b])} = ${N(v)} → ${term(m,'x',true)} = ${m!==1?`${N(v-b)} → <b>x = ${N(x)}</b>`:`<b>${N(x)}</b>`}.`};}
  const a=nz(-8,8),K=[[`√(${poly([1,-a])})`,`x ≥ ${N(a)}`,'The inside of a square root can’t be negative'],[`1/(${poly([1,-a])})`,`x ≠ ${N(a)}`,'The denominator can’t be zero'],[`√(${poly([-1,a])})`,`x ≤ ${N(a)}`,'The inside of a square root can’t be negative']];
  if(d===3)K.push([`1/√(${poly([1,-a])})`,`x > ${N(a)}`,'Inside the root must be positive, and not zero since it’s in a denominator']);
  const [fx,ok,why]=pick(K),all=[`x ≥ ${N(a)}`,`x > ${N(a)}`,`x ≤ ${N(a)}`,`x ≠ ${N(a)}`,'All real numbers'].filter(o=>o!==ok).sort(()=>Math.random()-.5).slice(0,3),c=mc([ok,...all],0);
  return {q:`What’s the domain of f(x) = ${fx}?`,type:'choice',options:c.options,ans:c.ans,sol:`${why}: <b>${ok}</b>.`};}}
]},
{name:'Systems of equations',lessons:[
{id:'sys2',title:'Systems of two equations',bloom:'Analyze',
 hint:'Elimination: scale the equations so one variable cancels when you add or subtract. Or solve one for a variable and substitute.',
 ce:{text:'A unit fundraiser sells adult tickets for $12 and child tickets for $8. They sold 20 tickets for $200 total. How many of each?',item:{q:'a + c = 20 and 12a + 8c = 200',type:'fields',fields:[{label:'adult (a)',ans:10},{label:'child (c)',ans:10}],sol:'c = 20 − a. 12a + 8(20 − a) = 200 → 4a + 160 = 200 → a = 10, c = 10. <b>10 and 10</b>.'}},
 gni:`<p>A solution has to satisfy <b>both</b> equations. On a graph, it’s where the lines cross.</p><div class="rule"><b>Substitution:</b> solve one equation for a variable, plug into the other.<br><b>Elimination:</b> multiply so one variable has opposite coefficients, then add.</div><div class="ex">2x + y = 7<br>x − y = 2<br>Add: 3x = 9 → x = 3, then y = 1</div><p>Parallel lines never cross (no solution). The same line twice means infinitely many solutions.</p>`,
 dev:'Two unknowns, two facts: mixes, splits, two-rate trips. Any time you’re told two totals, you’re looking at a system.',
 gen(d){const x=ri(-8,8),y=ri(-8,8);let a1,b1,a2,b2,D;do{a1=d===1?1:nz(-6,6);b1=nz(-6,6);a2=nz(-6,6);b2=nz(-6,6);D=a1*b2-b1*a2;}while(D===0);
  const c1=a1*x+b1*y,c2=a2*x+b2*y;
  return {q:`Solve the system:<div class="sys">${eqn([a1,b1],['x','y'],c1)}<br>${eqn([a2,b2],['x','y'],c2)}</div>`,type:'fields',fields:[{label:'x',ans:x},{label:'y',ans:y}],
   sol:`Using determinants: D = (${N(a1)})(${N(b2)}) − (${N(b1)})(${N(a2)}) = ${N(D)}. x = [(${N(c1)})(${N(b2)}) − (${N(b1)})(${N(c2)})]/D = ${N(x)}. y = [(${N(a1)})(${N(c2)}) − (${N(c1)})(${N(a2)})]/D = ${N(y)}. <b>(${N(x)}, ${N(y)})</b>.`};}},
{id:'sys3',title:'Three-variable systems',bloom:'Analyze',
 hint:'Use two different pairs of equations to eliminate the same variable. That leaves a 2×2 system.',
 ce:{text:'Three kit orders: 1 radio + 2 batteries + 1 antenna = $390. 2 radios + 1 battery + 3 antennas = $770. 1 radio + 4 batteries + 2 antennas = $480. Price each item.',item:{q:'r + 2b + a = 390<br>2r + b + 3a = 770<br>r + 4b + 2a = 480',type:'fields',fields:[{label:'radio r',ans:300},{label:'battery b',ans:20},{label:'antenna a',ans:50}],sol:'Eq3 − Eq1: 2b + a = 90. 2·Eq1 − Eq2: 3b − a = 10. Add: 5b = 100 → b = 20, a = 50, r = 390 − 40 − 50 = 300. <b>$300, $20, $50</b>.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule">Pick one variable. Eliminate it from two different pairs of equations. Now you have two equations in two unknowns. Solve, then back-substitute.</div><div class="rule"><b>Cramer’s rule:</b> x = Dₓ/D, where D is the determinant of the coefficients and Dₓ swaps the x column for the constants. (More in the Matrices task.)</div>`,
 dev:'Three unknowns show up in mixes, cost breakdowns, and position fixes. This is the doorway to how GPS and direction finding solve for location.',
 gen(){const s=[ri(-5,5),ri(-5,5),ri(-5,5)];let A,D;do{A=[0,1,2].map(()=>[0,1,2].map(()=>ri(-3,3)));D=det3(A);}while(D===0||A.some(r=>r.every(v=>v===0)));
  const c=A.map(r=>r[0]*s[0]+r[1]*s[1]+r[2]*s[2]),V=['x','y','z'],Dk=[0,1,2].map(j=>det3(A.map((r,i)=>r.map((v,k)=>k===j?c[i]:v))));
  return {q:`Solve:<div class="sys">${A.map((r,i)=>eqn(r,V,c[i])).join('<br>')}</div>`,type:'fields',fields:V.map((v,i)=>({label:v,ans:s[i]})),
   sol:`D = ${N(D)}. Dₓ = ${N(Dk[0])}, D<sub>y</sub> = ${N(Dk[1])}, D<sub>z</sub> = ${N(Dk[2])}. Divide each by D: <b>(${s.map(N).join(', ')})</b>.`};}}
]},
{name:'Exponents and radicals',lessons:[
{id:'exp',title:'Exponent rules',bloom:'Apply',
 hint:'Multiply: add exponents. Divide: subtract. Power of a power: multiply. Coefficients follow normal arithmetic.',
 ce:{text:'2¹⁰ bytes is 1 KiB, and 2¹⁰ KiB is 1 MiB. How many bytes in a MiB, as a power of 2?',item:{q:'2¹⁰ × 2¹⁰ = 2^?',type:'num',ans:20,sol:'Same base, multiply means add exponents: 10 + 10 = <b>20</b>. So 1 MiB = 2²⁰ bytes.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule">xᵃ · xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ &nbsp;&nbsp; xᵃ / xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ &nbsp;&nbsp; (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ<br>(xy)ⁿ = xⁿyⁿ &nbsp;&nbsp; x⁰ = 1 &nbsp;&nbsp; x⁻ⁿ = 1/xⁿ</div><div class="ex">(2x³)² · x⁻⁴<br>= 4x⁶ · x⁻⁴<br>= 4x²</div>`,
 dev:'Powers of 2 run every bit of IT, and powers of 10 run every unit prefix (kilo, mega, giga). Exponent rules are how you convert between them in your head.',
 gen(d){const a=ri(2,7),b=ri(1,6),p=ri(1,5),q=ri(1,5),f=pick(d===1?['prod','quot']:d===2?['prod','quot','pow']:['quot','pow','neg']),X=(c,e)=>`${c===1?'':c}x${sup(e)}`;
  if(f==='prod')return {q:`Simplify (${X(p,a)})(${X(q,b)}) into k·xⁿ.`,type:'fields',fields:[{label:'k',ans:p*q},{label:'n',ans:a+b}],sol:`Multiply coefficients: ${p*q}. Add exponents: ${a} + ${b} = ${a+b}. <b>${X(p*q,a+b)}</b>.`};
  if(f==='quot'){const b2=d===1?ri(1,a-1):ri(1,9);return {q:`Simplify ${X(p*q,a)} ÷ ${X(q,b2)} into k·xⁿ. (n can be zero or negative.)`,type:'fields',fields:[{label:'k',ans:p},{label:'n',ans:a-b2}],sol:`Divide coefficients: ${p*q} ÷ ${q} = ${p}. Subtract exponents: ${a} − ${b2} = ${N(a-b2)}. <b>${p}x${sup(a-b2)}</b>.`};}
  if(f==='pow'){const pp=ri(1,3),n=ri(2,3);return {q:`Simplify (${X(pp,a)})${sup(n)} into k·xⁿ.`,type:'fields',fields:[{label:'k',ans:pp**n},{label:'n',ans:a*n}],sol:`${pp>1?`${pp}${sup(n)} = ${pp**n}. `:''}Multiply exponents: ${a}·${n} = ${a*n}. <b>${X(pp**n,a*n)}</b>.`};}
  return {q:`Simplify ${X(p,a)} · x${sup(-b)} · x${sup(-a)} into k·xⁿ.`,type:'fields',fields:[{label:'k',ans:p},{label:'n',ans:-b}],sol:`Add exponents: ${a} + (${M}${b}) + (${M}${a}) = ${N(-b)}. <b>${X(p,-b)}</b>, which is ${p}/x${b>1?sup(b):''}.`};}},
{id:'rad',title:'Simplifying radicals',bloom:'Apply',
 hint:'Find the biggest perfect square factor (4, 9, 16, 25, 36…) and pull its root outside.',
 ce:{text:'A square tent floor is 6 m by 6 m. Its diagonal is √72 m. Write √72 as a√b.',item:{q:'√72 = a√b',type:'fields',fields:[{label:'a',ans:6},{label:'b',ans:2}],sol:'72 = 36 · 2, and √36 = 6. <b>6√2</b> ≈ 8.49 m.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule">√(ab) = √a · √b. Pull out perfect squares. Combine like radicals like like terms: 3√2 + 5√2 = 8√2.</div><div class="ex">√50 = √25 · √2 = 5√2<br>√12 + √27 = 2√3 + 3√3 = 5√3</div><div class="rule"><b>Rationalize:</b> 1/√2 = √2/2 (multiply top and bottom by √2).</div>`,
 dev:'Diagonals, distances, and RMS values come out as radicals. The simplified form shows you the structure, like a 45° diagonal always being side × √2.',
 gen(d){const B=[2,3,5,6,7,10,11];
  if(d===1||Math.random()<.35){const a=ri(2,9),b=pick(B);return {q:`Simplify √${a*a*b} into a√b.`,type:'fields',fields:[{label:'a',ans:a},{label:'b',ans:b}],sol:`${a*a*b} = ${a*a} · ${b}, and √${a*a} = ${a}. <b>${a}√${b}</b>.`};}
  let b,s,t,p,q;do{b=pick([2,3,5,6,7]);s=ri(1,5);t=ri(1,5);p=nz(-4,4);q=nz(-4,4);}while(p*s+q*t===0||s===t);
  const r1=s*s*b,r2=t*t*b,A=p*s+q*t;
  return {q:`Simplify ${cstr(p)}√${r1} ${q<0?M:'+'} ${Math.abs(q)===1?'':Math.abs(q)}√${r2} into a√b.`,type:'fields',fields:[{label:'a',ans:A},{label:'b',ans:b}],sol:`√${r1} = ${s===1?'':s}√${b} and √${r2} = ${t===1?'':t}√${b}. Combine: (${N(p*s)}${sg(q*t)})√${b} = <b>${N(A)}√${b}</b>.`};}},
{id:'ratexp',title:'Rational exponents and roots',bloom:'Apply',
 hint:'x^(m/n): take the nth root first, then raise to the m. A negative exponent means take the reciprocal.',
 ce:{text:'A cube-shaped storage crate holds 27 ft³. How long is each side? Side = 27^(1/3).',item:{q:'27<sup>1/3</sup> = ?',type:'num',ans:3,sol:'Cube root of 27 is <b>3 ft</b>, since 3 × 3 × 3 = 27.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule">x<sup>1/n</sup> = ⁿ√x &nbsp;&nbsp; x<sup>m/n</sup> = (ⁿ√x)<sup>m</sup> &nbsp;&nbsp; x<sup>−m/n</sup> = 1/x<sup>m/n</sup></div><div class="ex">16<sup>3/4</sup> = (⁴√16)³ = 2³ = 8<br>8<sup>−2/3</sup> = 1/(∛8)² = 1/4</div><p>Root first keeps the numbers small.</p>`,
 dev:'Rational exponents show up in scaling laws: area vs. volume, power vs. range, and the inverse-square law for signal and radiation.',
 gen(d){const n=pick(d===1?[2,3]:[2,3,4]),r=n===4?ri(2,3):ri(2,5);let m=ri(1,3);if(m%n===0)m=1;const neg=d===3&&Math.random()<.5,base=r**n,v=neg?1/r**m:r**m;
  return {q:`Evaluate ${base}<sup>${neg?M:''}${m}/${n}</sup>`,type:'num',ans:v,sol:`${n===2?'Square':n===3?'Cube':'4th'} root of ${base} is ${r}. ${r}${sup(m)} = ${r**m}.${neg?` Negative exponent: reciprocal → <b>1/${r**m}</b>.`:` <b>${r**m}</b>.`}`};}}
]},
{name:'Polynomials and factoring',lessons:[
{id:'polyops',title:'Polynomial operations',bloom:'Apply',
 hint:'Subtracting: distribute the minus to every term. Multiplying: every term times every term, then combine.',
 ce:{text:'A square ground plane has sides of (x + 3) cm. Write its area (x + 3)² as ax² + bx + c.',item:{q:'(x + 3)² = ax² + bx + c',type:'fields',fields:[{label:'a',ans:1},{label:'b',ans:6},{label:'c',ans:9}],sol:'(x + 3)(x + 3) = x² + 3x + 3x + 9 = <b>x² + 6x + 9</b>. (Not x² + 9.)'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule"><b>Add/subtract:</b> combine like terms. When subtracting, flip the sign of every term in the second polynomial.</div><div class="rule"><b>Multiply:</b> every term in the first times every term in the second (FOIL for two binomials).</div><div class="ex">(2x − 3)(x + 4)<br>= 2x² + 8x − 3x − 12<br>= 2x² + 5x − 12</div>`,
 dev:'Area and volume with unknown dimensions, and any model you combine or scale, end up as polynomial arithmetic.',
 gen(d){if(d===1){const A=[nz(-6,6),nz(-9,9),nz(-9,9)],B=[nz(-6,6),nz(-9,9),nz(-9,9)],s=pick([1,-1]),R=A.map((v,i)=>v+s*B[i]);
   return {q:`Simplify (${poly(A)}) ${s>0?'+':M} (${poly(B)}) into ax² + bx + c.`,type:'fields',fields:['a','b','c'].map((l,i)=>({label:l,ans:R[i]})),sol:`${s<0?'Flip every sign in the second, then combine':'Combine like terms'}: <b>${poly(R)}</b>.`};}
  if(d===2){const a=nz(-4,4),b=nz(-9,9),c=nz(-4,4),e=nz(-9,9),R=[a*c,a*e+b*c,b*e];return {q:`Multiply (${poly([a,b])})(${poly([c,e])}) into ax² + bx + c.`,type:'fields',fields:['a','b','c'].map((l,i)=>({label:l,ans:R[i]})),sol:`First ${N(a*c)}x², outer + inner ${N(a*e)}x${sg(b*c)}x, last ${N(b*e)}. <b>${poly(R)}</b>.`};}
  const a=nz(-3,3),b=nz(-5,5),c=nz(-3,3),e=nz(-5,5),f=nz(-5,5),R=[a*c,a*e+b*c,a*f+b*e,b*f];
  return {q:`Multiply (${poly([a,b])})(${poly([c,e,f])}) into ax³ + bx² + cx + d.`,type:'fields',fields:['a','b','c','d'].map((l,i)=>({label:l,ans:R[i]})),sol:`Distribute each term of the binomial across the trinomial, then combine: <b>${poly(R)}</b>.`};}},
{id:'polydiv',title:'Polynomial division and the remainder theorem',bloom:'Apply',
 hint:'Dividing f(x) by (x − a) leaves remainder f(a). For synthetic division, bring down, multiply by a, add, repeat.',
 ce:{text:'f(x) = x³ − 2x² + 5. Without long division, find the remainder when f(x) is divided by (x − 2).',item:{q:'Remainder = ?',type:'num',ans:5,sol:'Remainder theorem: f(2) = 8 − 8 + 5 = <b>5</b>.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule"><b>Remainder theorem:</b> f(x) ÷ (x − a) leaves remainder f(a).<br><b>Factor theorem:</b> if f(a) = 0, then (x − a) is a factor.</div><div class="ex">Synthetic division of x² + 5x + 7 by (x − 2):<br>bring down 1 → 1·2 + 5 = 7 → 7·2 + 7 = 21<br>quotient x + 7, remainder 21</div>`,
 dev:'This is how you test candidate roots fast, and the same idea (remainders) is behind CRC error checking on every network frame.',
 gen(d){if(d===1||Math.random()<.5){const c=[nz(-3,3),ri(-6,6),ri(-6,6),ri(-9,9)],a=nz(-3,3),f=v=>c[0]*v**3+c[1]*v*v+c[2]*v+c[3];
   return {q:`Find the remainder when f(x) = ${poly(c)} is divided by (${poly([1,-a])}).`,type:'num',ans:f(a),sol:`Remainder theorem: f(${N(a)}) = <b>${N(f(a))}</b>.`};}
  const r=nz(-6,6),b=ri(-8,8),c=ri(-12,12),s=b+r,rem=c+r*s;
  return {q:`Divide (${poly([1,b,c])}) by (${poly([1,-r])}). The quotient is x + q with remainder R.`,type:'fields',fields:[{label:'q',ans:s},{label:'R',ans:rem}],sol:`Synthetic with ${N(r)}: bring down 1; 1·${P(r)} + ${P(b)} = ${N(s)}; ${N(s)}·${P(r)} + ${P(c)} = ${N(rem)}. <b>Quotient ${poly([1,s])}, remainder ${N(rem)}</b>.`};}},
{id:'factor',title:'Factoring trinomials and GCF',bloom:'Analyze',
 hint:'Pull out the GCF first. For x² + bx + c, find two numbers that multiply to c and add to b.',
 ce:{text:'A TOC tent covers x² + 7x + 12 square meters. Factor it to find expressions for the length and width.',item:{q:'Factor: x² + 7x + 12',type:'expr',ans:'(x+3)(x+4)',minF:2,sol:'3 × 4 = 12 and 3 + 4 = 7. <b>(x + 3)(x + 4)</b>.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule"><b>Step 1:</b> always pull out the greatest common factor.<br><b>x² + bx + c:</b> find two numbers with product c and sum b.<br><b>ax² + bx + c (AC method):</b> find two numbers with product a·c and sum b, split the middle term, group.</div><div class="ex">2x² + 7x + 3<br>a·c = 6 → 6 and 1<br>2x² + 6x + x + 3<br>= 2x(x + 3) + 1(x + 3)<br>= (2x + 1)(x + 3)</div><p>Type it like <code>(2x+1)(x+3)</code>. Check by multiplying back out.</p>`,
 dev:'Factoring turns a hard question into easy ones: once it’s in pieces, finding zeros, dimensions, or break-even points is immediate.',
 gen(d){let k=1,a=1,p=nz(-9,9);const q=nz(-9,9);if(d>=2){a=pick([2,3,5]);do{p=nz(-9,9)}while(gcd(a,p)!==1);}if(d===3)k=pick([2,3,4,-2]);
  const co=[k*a,k*(a*q+p),k*p*q],pr=`${k===1?'':N(k)}(${poly([a,p])})(${poly([1,q])})`;
  return {q:`Factor completely: ${poly(co)}`,type:'expr',minF:2,ans:`${k===1?'':k}${fac(a,p)}${fac(1,q)}`,sol:`${k!==1?`Pull out GCF ${N(k)}. `:''}${a===1?`Two numbers with product ${N(p*q)} and sum ${N(p+q)}: ${N(p)} and ${N(q)}.`:`AC method: product ${N(a*p*q)}, sum ${N(a*q+p)} → ${N(a*q)} and ${N(p)}. Split and group.`} <b>${pr}</b>.`};}},
{id:'special',title:'Special factoring patterns',bloom:'Analyze',
 hint:'a² − b² = (a − b)(a + b). a² ± 2ab + b² = (a ± b)². Cubes: a³ ∓ b³ = (a ∓ b)(a² ± ab + b²).',
 ce:{text:'x² − 49 has no middle term. That’s a difference of squares. Factor it.',item:{q:'Factor: x² − 49',type:'expr',ans:'(x-7)(x+7)',minF:2,sol:'x² − 7² = <b>(x − 7)(x + 7)</b>.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule">a² − b² = (a − b)(a + b)<br>a² + 2ab + b² = (a + b)² &nbsp;&nbsp; a² − 2ab + b² = (a − b)²<br>a³ − b³ = (a − b)(a² + ab + b²)<br>a³ + b³ = (a + b)(a² − ab + b²)</div><div class="ex">9x² − 25 = (3x − 5)(3x + 5)<br>x² − 10x + 25 = (x − 5)²<br>x³ − 8 = (x − 2)(x² + 2x + 4)</div><p>a² + b² doesn’t factor over the real numbers.</p>`,
 dev:'Recognizing patterns on sight is the payoff. It’s the algebra version of knowing your immediate action drills cold.',
 gen(d){const f=pick(d===1?['dos','ps']:d===2?['dos','ps']:['dos','ps','cube']);
  if(f==='dos'){let a,b;do{a=ri(1,4);b=ri(1,9)}while(gcd(a,b)!==1);return {q:`Factor: ${poly([a*a,0,-b*b])}`,type:'expr',minF:2,ans:`${fac(a,-b)}${fac(a,b)}`,sol:`(${a===1?'':a}x)² − ${b}² → <b>(${poly([a,-b])})(${poly([a,b])})</b>.`};}
  if(f==='ps'){const b=ri(1,9),s=pick([1,-1]);return {q:`Factor: ${poly([1,2*s*b,b*b])}`,type:'expr',minF:2,ans:`${fac(1,s*b)}^2`,sol:`${b*b} = ${b}² and ${2*b} = 2·${b}. Perfect square: <b>(${poly([1,s*b])})²</b>.`};}
  const b=ri(1,5),s=pick([1,-1]);return {q:`Factor: ${poly([1,0,0,s*b**3])}`,type:'expr',minF:2,ans:`${fac(1,s*b)}(x^2${s<0?'+':'-'}${b}x+${b*b})`,sol:`${s<0?'Difference':'Sum'} of cubes with b = ${b}: <b>(${poly([1,s*b])})(${poly([1,-s*b,b*b])})</b>.`};}}
]},
{name:'Quadratics and complex numbers',lessons:[
{id:'quad',title:'Solving quadratic equations',bloom:'Apply',
 hint:'Set it equal to zero. Factor if you can; otherwise x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a.',
 ce:{text:'A round’s height is modeled by h = −5t² + 20t (meters, seconds). When is it on the ground (h = 0)? Give both times.',item:{q:'Solve: −5t² + 20t = 0',type:'set',ans:[0,4],sol:'Factor: −5t(t − 4) = 0, so t = 0 (launch) or t = 4 (impact). <b>0, 4</b>.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule"><b>Zero product:</b> if AB = 0, then A = 0 or B = 0. So factor and set each piece to zero.</div><div class="rule"><b>Quadratic formula</b> (always works): x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a</div><div class="ex">x² − x − 6 = 0<br>(x − 3)(x + 2) = 0<br>x = 3 or x = −2</div><p>Separate answers with commas. Decimals to 2 places are fine. So are exact forms like <code>(-3+sqrt(5))/2</code>.</p>`,
 dev:'Projectiles, areas, and anything that accelerates are quadratic. If you’ve ever computed time of flight, you’ve used this.',
 gen(d){if(d<3){const r1=ri(-9,9),r2=ri(-9,9),a=d===1?1:nz(-3,3),co=[a,-a*(r1+r2),a*r1*r2];
   return {q:`Solve: ${poly(co)} = 0`,type:'set',ans:[r1,r2],sol:`Factor: ${cstr(a)}(${poly([1,-r1])})(${poly([1,-r2])}) = 0. <b>x = ${r1===r2?N(r1)+' (double root)':N(r1)+', '+N(r2)}</b>.`};}
  let B,C,D;do{B=nz(-9,9);C=ri(-9,9);D=B*B-4*C;}while(D<=0||Number.isInteger(Math.sqrt(D)));const x1=(-B+Math.sqrt(D))/2,x2=(-B-Math.sqrt(D))/2;
  return {q:`Solve: ${poly([1,B,C])} = 0 &nbsp;<span class="dim">(2 decimal places)</span>`,type:'set',ans:[x1,x2],tol:.011,sol:`It doesn’t factor nicely. x = (${N(-B)} ± √${D})/2 ≈ <b>${r2(x1).replace('-',M)}, ${r2(x2).replace('-',M)}</b>.`};}},
{id:'disc',title:'The discriminant',bloom:'Analyze',
 hint:'D = b² − 4ac. Positive: two real roots. Zero: one repeated root. Negative: two complex roots.',
 ce:{text:'Before solving x² − 6x + 10 = 0, check whether it even has real solutions. Compute b² − 4ac.',item:{q:'b² − 4ac = ?',type:'num',ans:-4,sol:'(−6)² − 4(1)(10) = 36 − 40 = <b>−4</b>. Negative, so there are no real solutions; the roots are complex.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule">The <b>discriminant</b> D = b² − 4ac is the part under the root in the quadratic formula.<br>D &gt; 0 → two distinct real roots<br>D = 0 → one repeated real root<br>D &lt; 0 → two complex (non-real) roots</div><div class="ex">2x² + 3x − 5: D = 9 + 40 = 49 → two real roots</div>`,
 dev:'The discriminant answers “is this even possible?” before you do the work, like checking if a target is in range before computing a firing solution.',
 gen(d){const f=d===1?'calc':pick(['calc','type']),kind=pick(['pos','zero','neg']);let a,b,c,D;
  if(kind==='zero'){const r=nz(-6,6);a=pick([1,1,2]);b=-2*a*r;c=a*r*r;}else{do{a=nz(-4,4);b=ri(-9,9);c=ri(-9,9);D=b*b-4*a*c}while(kind==='pos'?D<=0:D>=0);}
  D=b*b-4*a*c;const s=`D = (${N(b)})² − 4(${N(a)})(${N(c)}) = ${N(D)}`;
  if(f==='calc')return {q:`Find the discriminant of ${poly([a,b,c])} = 0.`,type:'num',ans:D,sol:`<b>${s}</b>.`};
  const opts=['Two distinct real roots','One repeated real root','Two complex (non-real) roots'],i=D>0?0:D===0?1:2;
  return {q:`What kind of roots does ${poly([a,b,c])} = 0 have?`,type:'choice',options:opts,ans:i,sol:`${s}, so <b>${opts[i].toLowerCase()}</b>.`};}},
{id:'vertex',title:'Vertex form and graphing parabolas',bloom:'Analyze',
 hint:'h = −b/(2a), then k = f(h). In vertex form y = a(x − h)² + k, the vertex is (h, k).',
 ce:{text:'Drag h and k until the bottom of the parabola sits at (3, −2), with a = 1.',graph:{exprs:['a*(x-h)^2+k'],params:{a:1,h:0,k:0}},item:{q:'With the vertex at (3, −2) and a = 1, what is y when x = 5?',type:'num',ans:2,sol:'y = (5 − 3)² − 2 = 4 − 2 = <b>2</b>. Notice h moved the graph right, even though it’s written x − h.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule"><b>Vertex form:</b> y = a(x − h)² + k. Vertex (h, k), axis of symmetry x = h. a &gt; 0 opens up (minimum), a &lt; 0 opens down (maximum). |a| &gt; 1 is narrower.</div><div class="rule">From standard form y = ax² + bx + c: <b>h = −b/(2a)</b>, then k = y at x = h.</div><div class="ex">y = x² − 6x + 5<br>h = 6/2 = 3, k = 9 − 18 + 5 = −4<br>vertex (3, −4), so y = (x − 3)² − 4</div>`,
 dev:'The vertex is the max or min: peak height of a trajectory, best price point, lowest cost. Optimization starts here.',
 gen(d){const a=d===1?1:nz(-3,3),h=nz(-6,6),k=ri(-9,9),B=-2*a*h,C=a*h*h+k;
  return {q:`Find the vertex of y = ${poly([a,B,C])}.`,type:'fields',fields:[{label:'h (x of vertex)',ans:h},{label:'k (y of vertex)',ans:k}],sol:`h = −b/(2a) = ${N(-B)}/${N(2*a)} = ${N(h)}. k = y(${N(h)}) = ${N(k)}. <b>Vertex (${N(h)}, ${N(k)})</b>, a ${a>0?'minimum':'maximum'}.`};}},
{id:'cplx',title:'Complex numbers',bloom:'Apply',
 hint:'Treat i like a variable, then replace i² with −1. To divide, multiply top and bottom by the conjugate.',
 ce:{text:'In AC circuits, impedance is a complex number. A load of 3 + 4i Ω is in series with 2 − i Ω. Series impedances add. What’s the total?',item:{q:'(3 + 4i) + (2 − i) = ? + ?i',type:'fields',fields:[{label:'Real part',ans:5},{label:'Imaginary part',ans:3}],sol:'Add real parts and imaginary parts separately: <b>5 + 3i Ω</b>.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule">i = √−1, so i² = −1. A complex number is a + bi.<br>Powers cycle every 4: i, −1, −i, 1.</div><div class="ex">(2 + 3i)(1 − 4i)<br>= 2 − 8i + 3i − 12i²<br>= 2 − 5i + 12 = 14 − 5i</div><div class="rule"><b>Divide:</b> multiply by the conjugate of the bottom (c + di → c − di). The bottom becomes c² + d².</div>`,
 dev:'Engineers call it j, but it’s the same thing. Complex numbers describe phase and amplitude, which is how AC circuits, I/Q signals, and SDRs work.',
 gen(d){const f=pick(d===1?['add','pow']:d===2?['add','mul','pow']:['mul','div','pow']),C=(a,b)=>{if(b===0)return N(a);const bi=(Math.abs(b)===1?'':Math.abs(b))+'i';if(a===0)return (b<0?M:'')+bi;return `${N(a)} ${b<0?M:'+'} ${bi}`;},F=(r,i)=>[{label:'Real part',ans:r},{label:'Imaginary part',ans:i}];
  const a=nz(-7,7),b=nz(-7,7),c=nz(-7,7),e=nz(-7,7);
  if(f==='add'){const s=pick([1,-1]);return {q:`Compute (${C(a,b)}) ${s>0?'+':M} (${C(c,e)}).`,type:'fields',fields:F(a+s*c,b+s*e),sol:`Real: ${N(a)} ${s>0?'+':M} ${P(c)} = ${N(a+s*c)}. Imaginary: ${N(b)} ${s>0?'+':M} ${P(e)} = ${N(b+s*e)}. <b>${C(a+s*c,b+s*e)}</b>.`};}
  if(f==='mul')return {q:`Compute (${C(a,b)})(${C(c,e)}).`,type:'fields',fields:F(a*c-b*e,a*e+b*c),sol:`FOIL: ${N(a*c)}${sg(a*e)}i${sg(b*c)}i${sg(b*e)}i². With i² = −1: <b>${C(a*c-b*e,a*e+b*c)}</b>.`};
  if(f==='pow'){const n=ri(5,60),cyc=[[1,0],[0,1],[-1,0],[0,-1]][n%4];return {q:`Compute i${sup(n)}.`,type:'fields',fields:F(cyc[0],cyc[1]),sol:`${n} ÷ 4 leaves remainder ${n%4}, so i${sup(n)} = i${sup(n%4)} = <b>${C(cyc[0],cyc[1])}</b>.`};}
  const p=nz(-5,5),q=nz(-5,5),cc=nz(-4,4),dd=nz(-4,4),nr=p*cc-q*dd,ni=p*dd+q*cc;
  return {q:`Compute (${C(nr,ni)}) ÷ (${C(cc,dd)}).`,type:'fields',fields:F(p,q),sol:`Multiply top and bottom by the conjugate ${C(cc,-dd)}. Bottom: ${cc*cc} + ${dd*dd} = ${cc*cc+dd*dd}. Result: <b>${C(p,q)}</b>.`};}}
]},
{name:'Rational and radical equations',lessons:[
{id:'ratx',title:'Rational expressions',bloom:'Apply',
 hint:'Factor top and bottom, cancel common factors (not terms), and note what makes the original denominator zero.',
 ce:{text:'Simplify (x² − 9)/(x − 3). The result looks like x + c. Also, which x-value is excluded?',item:{q:'(x² − 9)/(x − 3)',type:'fields',fields:[{label:'c in x + c',ans:3},{label:'excluded x',ans:3}],sol:'(x − 3)(x + 3)/(x − 3) = <b>x + 3</b>, with <b>x ≠ 3</b> because the original denominator would be zero.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule">Factor, then cancel <b>factors</b>, never terms. (x + 2)/2 is not x + 1.</div><div class="rule"><b>Excluded values</b> come from the original denominator, even after canceling.</div><div class="ex">(x² + 5x + 6)/(x + 2)<br>= (x + 2)(x + 3)/(x + 2)<br>= x + 3, x ≠ −2</div><p>Adding rational expressions works like fractions: get a common denominator first.</p>`,
 dev:'Rates combined with rates (per-unit costs, combined resistances, shared work) produce rational expressions. Simplifying keeps them manageable.',
 gen(d){const p=nz(-9,9),q=ri(-9,9),k=d===3?pick([2,3,-2]):1,num=[k,k*(p+q),k*p*q];
  return {q:`Simplify (${poly(num)}) / (${poly([1,p])}) into mx + c, and give the excluded value.`,type:'fields',fields:[{label:'m',ans:k},{label:'c',ans:k*q},{label:'excluded x',ans:-p}],sol:`Factor the top: ${k===1?'':N(k)}(${poly([1,p])})(${poly([1,q])}). Cancel (${poly([1,p])}): <b>${poly([k,k*q])}</b>, with <b>x ≠ ${N(-p)}</b>.`};}},
{id:'radeq',title:'Rational and radical equations',bloom:'Apply',
 hint:'Radicals: isolate, then square both sides. Rationals: multiply by the denominator. Always check for extraneous answers.',
 ce:{text:'Crew A can do a job in 6 hours. Crew B can do it in 3 hours. Working together, how long does it take? Rates add: 1/6 + 1/3 = 1/t.',item:{q:'t = ? hours',type:'num',ans:2,sol:'1/6 + 2/6 = 3/6 = 1/2, so 1/t = 1/2 and <b>t = 2 hours</b>.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule"><b>Rational:</b> multiply both sides by the LCD, solve, then reject any answer that makes a denominator zero.</div><div class="rule"><b>Radical:</b> isolate the root, raise both sides to the power, solve, then <b>check</b>. Squaring can create fake (extraneous) solutions.</div><div class="ex">√(2x + 3) = x<br>2x + 3 = x²<br>x² − 2x − 3 = 0 → x = 3 or −1<br>Check −1: √1 = 1 ≠ −1 ✗ → x = 3</div>`,
 dev:'Combined work rates, parallel resistors, and distance formulas all lead here. The check step is your quality control.',
 gen(d){const f=pick(d===1?['rad']:d===2?['rad','rat']:['rad','rat','work']);
  if(f==='rad'){const x=ri(-5,12),a=pick([1,2,3,4,5]),c=ri(1,9),b=c*c-a*x;return {q:`Solve: √(${poly([a,b])}) = ${c}`,type:'num',ans:x,sol:`Square both sides: ${poly([a,b])} = ${c*c}. Solve: <b>x = ${N(x)}</b>. Check: √${c*c} = ${c} ✓.`};}
  if(f==='rat'){const k=nz(-5,5),j=nz(-6,6),h=nz(-8,8),m=k*j,x=h+j;return {q:`Solve: ${N(m)}/(${poly([1,-h])}) = ${N(k)}`,type:'num',ans:x,sol:`Multiply by (${poly([1,-h])}): ${N(m)} = ${N(k)}(${poly([1,-h])}). So ${poly([1,-h])} = ${N(j)}, <b>x = ${N(x)}</b> (not ${N(h)}, so it’s allowed).`};}
  const [A,B,t]=pick([[3,6,2],[4,12,3],[6,12,4],[10,15,6],[12,24,8],[5,20,4],[20,30,12],[12,12,6]]);
  return {q:`Crew A finishes a job in ${A} h. Crew B takes ${B} h. Together, how many hours?`,type:'num',ans:t,sol:`1/${A} + 1/${B} = (${B} + ${A})/${A*B} = 1/t → t = ${A*B}/${A+B} = <b>${t} h</b>.`};}}
]},
{name:'Functions II',lessons:[
{id:'comp',title:'Composition and inverse functions',bloom:'Apply',
 hint:'f(g(x)): work inside out. Inverse: swap x and y, solve for y.',
 ce:{text:'f(c) = 1.8c + 32 converts Celsius to Fahrenheit. Water boils at 212 °F. Use the inverse to get Celsius.',item:{q:'f⁻¹(212) = ?',type:'num',ans:100,sol:'Solve 1.8c + 32 = 212 → 1.8c = 180 → <b>c = 100 °C</b>.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule"><b>Composition:</b> (f ∘ g)(x) = f(g(x)). Evaluate the inside function first.</div><div class="rule"><b>Inverse:</b> f⁻¹ undoes f. Swap x and y, then solve for y. Only one-to-one functions have inverses (horizontal line test).</div><div class="ex">f(x) = 3x − 6<br>x = 3y − 6<br>y = (x + 6)/3 → f⁻¹(x) = (x + 6)/3</div>`,
 dev:'Unit conversions chain together (composition) and run backward (inverse). Converting dBm to watts and back is exactly this.',
 gen(d){const f=pick(d===1?['comp']:d===2?['comp','invv']:['comp','invv','inve']),a=nz(-5,5),b=nz(-9,9);
  if(f==='comp'){const c=nz(-3,3),e=ri(-6,6),k=ri(-4,4),g=c*k*k+e,v=a*g+b;return {q:`f(x) = ${poly([a,b])} and g(x) = ${poly([c,0,e])}. Find f(g(${N(k)})).`,type:'num',ans:v,sol:`Inside first: g(${N(k)}) = ${N(g)}. Then f(${N(g)}) = <b>${N(v)}</b>.`};}
  if(f==='invv'){const m=ri(-8,8),v=a*m+b;return {q:`f(x) = ${poly([a,b])}. Find f⁻¹(${N(v)}).`,type:'num',ans:m,sol:`f⁻¹(${N(v)}) is the x where f(x) = ${N(v)}: ${poly([a,b])} = ${N(v)} → <b>x = ${N(m)}</b>.`};}
  return {q:`f(x) = ${poly([a,b])}. Write f⁻¹(x).`,type:'expr',ans:`(x-(${b}))/(${a})`,sol:`Swap: x = ${poly([a,b],'y')}. Solve: <b>f⁻¹(x) = (${poly([1,-b])})/${P(a)}</b>.`};}},
{id:'trans',title:'Function transformations',bloom:'Analyze',
 hint:'Inside the function (x − h) moves right h. Outside + k moves up k. A negative multiplier reflects over the x-axis.',
 ce:{text:'The first curve is the parent y = x². The second is y = (x − h)² + k. Set h = 4 and k = 0 and watch the second curve.',graph:{exprs:['x^2','(x-h)^2+k'],params:{h:0,k:0}},item:(()=>{const c=mc(['Right 4','Left 4','Up 4','Down 4'],0);return {q:'Compared to the parent, the second curve moved:',type:'choice',options:c.options,ans:c.ans,sol:'(x − 4) inside the function moves it <b>right 4</b>. It feels backwards: the input has to be 4 bigger to get the same output.'};})()},
 gni:`<div class="rule">f(x − h): right h &nbsp;&nbsp; f(x + h): left h<br>f(x) + k: up k &nbsp;&nbsp; f(x) − k: down k<br>−f(x): reflect over the x-axis &nbsp;&nbsp; f(−x): reflect over the y-axis<br>a·f(x): vertical stretch (|a| &gt; 1) or compress (|a| &lt; 1)</div><div class="ex">g(x) = −2(x + 1)² + 3<br>left 1, reflect, stretch ×2, up 3</div>`,
 dev:'Once you know a parent shape, every variant is a shift or stretch. This is reading a graph the way you’d read terrain: by its features, not point by point.',
 gen(d){const PR=[['x²',s=>`(${s})²`],['|x|',s=>`|${s}|`],['√x',s=>`√(${s})`],['x³',s=>`(${s})³`]],[pf,g]=pick(PR),h=nz(-6,6),k=nz(-6,6),a=d===3?pick([-1,2,-2,3,-3]):1;
  const fl=[{label:'horizontal shift (right +, left −)',ans:h},{label:'vertical shift (up +, down −)',ans:k}];if(d===3)fl.push({label:'stretch factor a (negative = reflected)',ans:a});
  return {q:`f(x) = ${pf}. g(x) = ${cstr(a)}${g(poly([1,-h]))}${sg(k)}. Describe g as a transformation of f.`,type:'fields',fields:fl,
   sol:`Inside (${poly([1,-h])}) → <b>${h>0?'right':'left'} ${Math.abs(h)}</b>. Outside ${sg(k).trim()} → <b>${k>0?'up':'down'} ${Math.abs(k)}</b>.${a!==1?` Factor ${N(a)} → ${a<0?'reflect over the x-axis':''}${a<0&&Math.abs(a)>1?' and ':''}${Math.abs(a)>1?'stretch ×'+Math.abs(a):''}.`:''}`};}}
]},
{name:'Exponential and logarithmic functions',lessons:[
{id:'expg',title:'Exponential growth and decay',bloom:'Apply',
 hint:'y = a·bᵗ. Growth by r%: b = 1 + r. Decay by r%: b = 1 − r. Compound: A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt).',
 ce:{text:'A cable run loses 20% of its signal power per 100 m section. You start with 50 W. What’s left after 3 sections?',item:{q:'P = 50(0.8)³ = ? W',type:'num',ans:25.6,tol:.011,sol:'Each section keeps 80%: 50 × 0.8 × 0.8 × 0.8 = <b>25.6 W</b>. Not 50 − 3(10) = 20.'}},
 gni:`<div class="rule"><b>Exponential:</b> y = a·bᵗ. a is the start, b is the factor per period. b &gt; 1 grows, 0 &lt; b &lt; 1 decays.</div><div class="rule">Compound interest: A = P(1 + r/n)<sup>nt</sup> &nbsp;&nbsp; Continuous: A = Pe<sup>rt</sup><br>Half-life: A = A₀(½)<sup>t/h</sup></div><div class="ex">$2,000 at 5% compounded monthly for 3 years<br>= 2000(1 + 0.05/12)³⁶ ≈ $2,322.94</div><p>Round to the nearest hundredth.</p>`,
 dev:'TSP growth, battery drain, signal loss per segment, and radioactive decay are all exponential. Percent-per-period never adds; it multiplies.',
